{"surah": 87, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "سَبِّحِ ٱسْمَ رَبِّكَ ٱلْأَعْلَى ", "translation": "Exalt the name of your Lord, the Most High,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَبِّحِ", "translit": "ِḥِّbَs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱسْمَ", "translit": "َmْsٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْلَى", "translit": "àَlْʿعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "علو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ فَسَوَّىٰ ", "translation": "Who created and proportioned", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَسَوَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّwَsَf", "root": "سوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَٱلَّذِى قَدَّرَ فَهَدَىٰ ", "translation": "And who destined and [then] guided", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَدَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّdَq", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَهَدَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdَhَf", "root": "هدي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَٱلَّذِىٓ أَخْرَجَ ٱلْمَرْعَىٰ ", "translation": "And who brings out the pasture", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَخْرَجَ", "translit": "َjgَrْhkَaʾ", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَرْعَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَʿعْrَmْlٱ", "root": "رعي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "فَجَعَلَهُۥ غُثَآءً أَحْوَىٰ ", "translation": "And [then] makes it black stubble.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَجَعَلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlَʿعَjgَf", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "غُثَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَhtُغ", "root": "غثو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَحْوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwْḥَaʾ", "root": "حوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "سَنُقْرِئُكَ فَلَا تَنسَىٰٓ ", "translation": "We will make you recite, [O Muḥammad], and you will not forget,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَنُقْرِئُكَ", "translit": "َkُyʾِrْqُnَs", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَنسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَsnَt", "root": "نسي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "إِلَّا مَا شَآءَ ٱللَّهُۚ إِنَّهُۥ يَعْلَمُ ٱلْجَهْرَ وَمَا يَخْفَىٰ ", "translation": "Except what Allāh should will. Indeed, He knows what is declared and what is hidden.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "شَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْجَهْرَ", "translit": "َrْhَjgْlٱ", "root": "جهر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَخْفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfْhkَy", "root": "خفي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَنُيَسِّرُكَ لِلْيُسْرَىٰ ", "translation": "And We will ease you toward ease.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنُيَسِّرُكَ", "translit": "َkُrِّsَyُnَw", "root": "يسر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلْيُسْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْsُyْlِl", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "فَذَكِّرْ إِن نَّفَعَتِ ٱلذِّكْرَىٰ ", "translation": "So remind, if the reminder should benefit;[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَذَكِّرْ", "translit": "ْrِّkَhdَf", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَّفَعَتِ", "translit": "ِtَʿعَfَّn", "root": "نفع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلذِّكْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْkِّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "سَيَذَّكَّرُ مَن يَخْشَىٰ ", "translation": "He who fears [Allāh] will be reminded.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَيَذَّكَّرُ", "translit": "ُrَّkَّhdَyَs", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَخْشَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhsْhkَy", "root": "خشي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "وَيَتَجَنَّبُهَا ٱلْأَشْقَى ", "translation": "But the wretched one will avoid it –", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَتَجَنَّبُهَا", "translit": "āَhُbَّnَjgَtَyَw", "root": "جنب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَشْقَى", "translit": "àَqْhsَaʾْlٱ", "root": "شقو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "ٱلَّذِى يَصْلَى ٱلنَّارَ ٱلْكُبْرَىٰ ", "translation": "[He] who will [enter and] burn in the greatest Fire,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَصْلَى", "translit": "àَlْṣَy", "root": "صلي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارَ", "translit": "َrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكُبْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْbُkْlٱ", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "ثُمَّ لَا يَمُوتُ فِيهَا وَلَا يَحْيَىٰ ", "translation": "Neither dying therein nor living.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَمُوتُ", "translit": "ُtwُmَy", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَحْيَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَyْḥَy", "root": "حيي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "قَدْ أَفْلَحَ مَن تَزَكَّىٰ ", "translation": "He has certainly succeeded who purifies himself", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَفْلَحَ", "translit": "َḥَlْfَaʾ", "root": "فلح"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَزَكَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّkَzَt", "root": "زكو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "وَذَكَرَ ٱسْمَ رَبِّهِۦ فَصَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "And mentions the name of his Lord and prays.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَذَكَرَ", "translit": "َrَkَhdَw", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱسْمَ", "translit": "َmْsٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَصَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَṣَf", "root": "صلو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "بَلْ تُؤْثِرُونَ ٱلْحَيَوٰةَ ٱلدُّنْيَا ", "translation": "But you prefer the worldly life,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُؤْثِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِhtْwʾُt", "root": "اثر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "وَٱلْءَاخِرَةُ خَيْرٌ وَأَبْقَىٰٓ ", "translation": "While the Hereafter is better and more enduring.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلْءَاخِرَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَrِhkāَءْlٱَw", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَأَبْقَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَqْbَaʾَw", "root": "بقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "إِنَّ هَٰذَا لَفِى ٱلصُّحُفِ ٱلْأُولَىٰ ", "translation": "Indeed, this is in the former scriptures,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَفِى", "translit": "àِfَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصُّحُفِ", "translit": "ِfُḥُّṣlٱ", "root": "صحف"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأُولَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlwُaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "صُحُفِ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ وَمُوسَىٰ ", "translation": "The scriptures of Abraham and Moses.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "صُحُفِ", "translit": "ِfُḥُṣ", "root": "صحف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَمُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُmَw", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "بعد العشاء بثلاث ركعات يقرأ في الأولى سبح اسم ربك الأعلى وفي الثانية قل يا أيها الكافرون وفي الثالثة قل هو الله أحد (8) وجاء في الخبر أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم كان يصلي بعد الوتر ركعتين جالسا وفي بعضها متربعا (9) وفي بعض الأخبار إذا أراد أن يدخل فراشه زحف إليه وصلى فوقه ركعتين قبل أن يرقد يقراء فيهما إذا زلزلت الأرض وسورة التكاثر (10) وفي رواية أخرى قل يا أيها الكافرون ويجوز الوتر مفصولا وموصولا بتسليمة واحدة وتسليمتين وقد أوتر رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بركعة (1) وثلاث (2) وخمس (3) وهكذا با", "english": "**The Sixth: The Witr Prayer.** Anas ibn Mālik said: \"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to perform the Witr after the evening prayer (ʿishāʾ) with three rakʿahs. He would recite in the first: {«سَبِّحِ اسْمَ رَبِّكَ الْأَعْلَى»} ('Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High'), and in the second: {«قُلْ يَا أَيُّهَا الْكَافِرُونَ»} ('Say: O you disbelievers'), and in the third: {«قُلْ هُوَ اللَّهُ أَحَدٌ»} ('Say: He is Allah, the One')\" (8). And it has come in the report that he, peace and blessings be upon him, used to pray two rakʿahs sitting after the Witr, and in some of them, sitting cross-legged (mutarabbīʿan) (9). And in some of the reports: \"When he wished to enter his bed, he would crawl to it and pray upon it two rakʿahs before he slept, reciting in them {«إِذَا زُلْزِلَتِ الْأَرْضُ»} ('When the earth is shaken') and Sūrat al-Takāthur (10).\" And in another narration: {«قُلْ يَا أَيُّهَا الْكَافِرُونَ»} ('Say: O you disbelievers').", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 157", "section_title_en": "The First Division: Acts That Recur with the Recurrence of Days and Nights — Eight in Number: Five Being the Regular Supererogatory Prayers (Rawātib) of the Five Obligatory Prayers, and Three Beyond Them: The Forenoon Prayer (Ḍuḥā), Reviving the Time Between the Two Evening Prayers, and the Night Vigil (Tahajjud)", "section_index": 157, "anchors": "- Root: وتر (w-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The odd number, the single; that which is separate from the even (shafʿ); also the root of \"to harm\" (watara) — to strike singly, to leave bereft.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوتر في العدد خلاف الشفع\" — the odd in number is the contrary of the even; \"وأوتر في الصلاة\" — and to perform the Witr prayer; \"التواتر: تتابع الشيء وترا وفرادى\" — succession of a thing singly and one by one.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (Literal): \"تواترت كتبه وواترها\" — his letters came successively; \"وأوتر: صلى الوتر\" — and awtara: he prayed the Witr. Majaz (Metaphorical): \"وترته حقه\" — I deprived him of his right; \"كأنما وتر أهله وماله\" — as if he had been bereft of his family and wealth.\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completion, fulfillment, reaching the full measure; the full and complete.\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — al-wāfī: that which has attained completion; \"وفى بعهده يفي وفاء، وأوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — he fulfilled his covenant, and awfā: when he completed the covenant and did not break its observance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (Literal): \"درهم واف. وكيل واف\" — a full dirham, a full measure. Majaz (Metaphorical): \"أوفى على المائة إذا زاد عليها\" — he surpassed the hundred when he exceeded it; \"وتوفي فلان، وتوفاه الله تعالى\" — he died, and Allah took him in death.\n- Root: ركع (r-k-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Bowing, inclining; the specific posture of bowing in prayer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الركوع: الانحناء، فتارة يستعمل في الهيئة المخصوصة في الصلاة كما هي، وتارة في التواضع والتذلل\" — al-rukūʿ: bowing; sometimes used for the specific posture in prayer as it is, and sometimes for humility and self-abasement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (Literal): \"شيخ راكع: منحن من الكبر\" — an old man rākiʿ: bent over from age; \"ومنها ركوع الصلاة\" — and from it is the bowing of prayer. Majaz (Metaphorical): \"لغبت الإبل حتى ركعت\" — the camels became weary until they bowed their heads and collapsed on their faces.\n- Root: وهي (w-h-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A rent or fissure in leather or cloth; weakness, slackening, loosening of a bond.\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الوهي: شق في الأديم والثوب ونحوهما\" — al-wahī: a split in hide and garment and the like; \"وكل شيء استرخى رباطه فقد وهي\" — and everything whose binding slackens has become wāhin (weak, rent).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (Literal): \"وهى الحائط. وفي الثوب والأديم وهي\" — the wall weakened; in the garment and hide there is a rent. Majaz (Metaphorical): \"قولهم للسحاب: واهي العزالي\" — their saying of the cloud: weak of the water-spouts, when it bursts with rain.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the intervening material constitutes a complete, meaningful unit, as in the construction \"بعد العشاء بثلاث ركعات\" where the prepositional phrase is completed by the adverbial qualification.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "7": [{"arabic": "علامات المرض وبهذا يعرف أن القلوب كلها مريضة إلا ما شاء الله إلا أن من الأمراض ما لا يعرفها صاحبها ومرض القلب مما لا يعرفه صاحبه فلذلك يغفل عنه وإن عرفه صعب عليه الصبر على مرارة دوائه فإن دواءه مخالفة الشهوات وهو نزع الروح فإن وجد من نفسه قوة الصبر عليه لم يجد طبيبا حاذقا يعالجه فإن الأطباء هم العلماء وقد استولى عليهم المرض فالطبيب المريض قلما يلتفت إلى علاجه فلهذا صار الداء عضالا والمرض مزمنا واندرس هذا العلم وأنكر بالكلية طب القلوب وأنكر مرضها وأقبل الخلق على حب الدنيا وعلى أعمال ظاهرها عبادات وباطنها", "english": "I say: The soul (nafs) that belongs to the human being — that by which he is distinguished from the beasts — for he is not distinguished from them by the power of eating, copulation, sight, or any other such thing. Rather, he is distinguished by the knowledge of things as they truly are. And the Origin of all things, their Existentiator and their Inventor, is God, Mighty and Majestic — He who made them things. So if one knew everything but did not know God, Mighty and Majestic, it would be as though he knew nothing at all. And the sign of knowledge is love: whoever knows God Most High loves Him. And the sign of love is that one does not prefer over Him this world nor any other beloved thing. As God Most High said: {«Say: If your fathers, your sons, your brothers, your spouses, your clans — the wealth you have acquired, commerce whose decline you fear, and dwellings you find pleasing — are more beloved to you than God and His Messenger and striving in His cause, then wait until God brings about His command»} [Q 9:24]. So whoever possesses something more beloved to him than God — his heart is sick. Just as any stomach to which clay has become more beloved than bread and water, or whose appetite for bread and water has fallen away — it is sick. These are the signs of sickness. And by this it is known that all hearts are sick — except what God wills. Yet among sicknesses there are those the sufferer does not recognize; and the sickness of the heart is among those the sufferer does not recognize. For this reason he is heedless of it. And even if he recognized it, patience upon the bitterness of its medicine would be difficult for him — for its medicine is opposing the appetites, and that is the tearing out of the spirit (nafs). And if he found in himself the strength of patience upon it, he would not find a skillful physician to treat him — for the physicians are the scholars, and sickness has overcome them. So the sick physician rarely attends to his own cure. For this reason the malady has become chronic and the sickness chronic. This science has become effaced, and the medicine of hearts is denied entirely, and the sickness of the heart is denied. And creation has turned to the love of this world and to deeds whose outward form is acts of worship but whose inward reality is habit and ostentation. These are the signs of the roots of sicknesses.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 624", "section_title_en": "The Soul's Distinction, the Sickness of the Heart, and the Path of Moderation", "section_index": 624, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that by which a thing is distinguished in its essential reality\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul (nafs) as the principle of rational discrimination and knowledge, distinguishing humans from beasts\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self or person; Metaphorical: the rational soul as the seat of knowledge and moral responsibility\n- Root: مرض (m-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Departure from the state of health and equilibrium\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Sickness of the heart as a metaphor for moral and spiritual vices — ignorance, cowardice, avarice, hypocrisy\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily illness; Metaphorical: spiritual malady whereby the heart inclines toward what is harmful and away from what is beneficial\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; that which is ever-turning and fluctuating between states\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart as the seat of understanding and moral perception; its \"turning\" (taqlīb) signifies its instability between soundness and sickness\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical organ; Metaphorical: the spiritual faculty of cognition and moral discernment\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measured, proportioned disposition; character and temperament\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Moral character as the inward form (ṣūrah bāṭinah) corresponding to the outward physical form\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: measuring and proportioning; Metaphorical: the ingrained disposition that governs action\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive constructions to isolate the defining characteristic of the human soul as knowledge of God, not animal appetites", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "16": [{"arabic": "مشاهدة العواقب ولذلك قال تعالى بل تؤثرون الحياة الدنيا والآخرة خير وأبقى وقال عز وجل كلا بل تحبون العاجلة وتذرون الآخرة فمن هذا حده فينبغي أن يعالج قلبه من حب الجاه بالعلم بالآفات العاجلة وهو أن يتفكر في الأخطار التي يستهدف لها أرباب الجاه في الدنيا فإن كل ذي جاه محسود ومقصود بالإيذاء وخائف على الدوام على جاهه ومحترز من أن تتغير منزلته في القلوب والقلوب أشد تغيرا من القدر في غليانها وهي مترددة بين الإقبال والإعراض فكل ما يبنى على قلوب الخلق يضاهي ما يبنى على أمواج البحر فإنه لا ثبات له والاشتغال بمراعاة القلوب وحفظ ال", "english": "And it is as though you had never ceased to be in the Hereafter — for these people, their attention was turned to the final outcome, and so their deeds were performed for its sake, through godfearingness (taqwā), since they knew that the final outcome belongs to the godfearing. Thus they held in contempt rank and wealth in this world. But the sight of most creatures is weak, confined to the immediate (al-ʿājilah), whose light does not extend to the witnessing of final outcomes. For this reason, Allah Most High said: {«بل تؤثرون الحياة الدنيا والآخرة خير وأبقى»} (\"Nay, you prefer the life of this world, while the Hereafter is better and more enduring\") [Al-Aʿlā 87:16–17]. And He, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«كلا بل تحبون العاجلة وتذرون الآخرة»} (\"Nay, but you love the immediate and forsake the Hereafter\") [Al-Qiyāmah 75:20–21].", "book_title": "Condemnation of Status and Ostentation", "section_title_ar": "Section 804", "section_title_en": "The Cure for Love of Status — Section 804 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 804, "anchors": "- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الجاه: القدر والمنزلة، وجاه عند الناس: وقع وعظم\" — rank, standing, and elevation in the eyes of others.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: jāh is a social accident (ʿaraḍ) attached to the estimations of hearts, not a real attribute of the self; its pursuit is a veiling distraction from the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"جاه عند السلطان: منزلته ووجهته.\" Metaphorical (majāz): the \"face\" (wajh) one presents to people — i.e., honor is but a reflected image in fluctuating hearts, like a building upon waves.\n- Root: خطر (khaṭar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخطر: الإشراف على الهلاك، والخطر: السبق الذي يراهن عليه\" — peril and stakes.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The \"danger\" (khaṭar) of status is that its possessor becomes a target (gharaḍ) for envy and harm — a Quranic motif of the world's transient allure versus the abiding Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خاطر بنفسه: ألقاها في المهلكة.\" Metaphorical: the aspirant to jāh \"stakes\" his own peace as wager in a game he cannot win.\n- Root: خمل (khamūl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخمول: خفاء الذكر وعدم الشهرة، وخمل الرجل: سقط ولم يُعرف\" — obscurity and anonymity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Obscurity (khamūl) is the therapeutic counterweight to jāh; it severs the heart's attachment to created estimations.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خمل الثوب: ما خفي من نسجه.\" Metaphorical: the soul's \"hidden weave\" — its true worth lies unseen, like the inner thread of cloth.\n- Root: قنع (qanāʿah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"قنع: رضي بالكفاف واكتفى باليسير\" — contentment with sufficiency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qanāʿah is the root that severs ṭamaʿ (covetous expectation) from creatures, redirecting hope solely to the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قنع الصائد: نصب القنوع\" (the hunter's blind). Metaphorical: the contented man \"blinds\" himself to people's opinions, as one hides in a blind to escape prey's notice.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the contrastive structure \"إنما يعالج بالعلم... وأما من حيث العمل\" where the particle ḥaṣr restricts each cure to its proper domain (knowledge vs. practice), and \"فإن\" clauses provide causal grounding for periodic sentences.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Status and Ostentation'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "{بل تؤثرون الحياة الدنيا} ثم بين أن الشر قديم في الطباع وأن ذلك مذكور في الكتب السالفة فقال {إن هذا لفي الصحف الأولى صحف إبراهيم وموسى} فهذا منهاج رياضة المريد وتربيته في التدريج إلى لقاء الله تعالى فأما تفصيل الرياضة في كل صفة فسيأتي فإن أغلب الصفات على الإنسان بطنه وفرجه ولسانه أعني به الشهوات المتعلقة بها ثم الغضب الذي هو كالجند لحماية الشهوات ثم مهما أحب الإنسان شهوة البطن والفرج وأنس بهما أحب الدنيا ولم يتمكن منها إلا بالمال والجاه وإذا طلب المال والجاه حدث فيه الكبر", "english": "Whoever devotes himself exclusively to remembrance (dhikr) and casts off the distracting attachments from his heart will not be free of such thoughts, for he has embarked upon a ship of peril. If he is saved, he becomes one of the kings of religion; if he errs, he becomes one of the perished. For this reason the Prophet (peace and blessing be upon him) said: \"Upon you is the religion of the old women\" {«عليكم بدين العجائز»}—that is, receiving the foundation of faith and the outward creed by way of blind acceptance (taqlīd), and occupying oneself with works of goodness. For the peril in deviating from that is great.", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 637", "section_title_en": "The Way of Spiritual Discipline: On the Perils of the Path and the Training of the Seeker (Section 637)", "section_index": 637, "anchors": "- Root: طرق (ṭ-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الطريق: السبيل الذي يطرق بالأرجل\" — the path beaten by feet; striking, knocking (as of a door at night).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطريق: السبيل الذي يطرق بالأرجل... وعنه استعير كل مسلك يسلكه الإنسان في فعل\" — every course a man pursues, whether praiseworthy or blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: striking iron with a hammer, knocking at a door. Metaphorical: \"طرقنا فلان طروقا... وطرقني الخيال\" — a visitor coming by night; \"أصابته طارقة من الطوارق\" — calamities that strike suddenly. Here, the \"مهالك الطريق\" are the perilous passes of the spiritual path that strike the seeker unawares.\n- Root: هلك (h-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perishing, destruction, ruin.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الهلاك على ثلاثة أوجه\" — three modes: (1) loss of a thing from one's possession while it exists elsewhere {هلك عني سلطانيه}; (2) corruption and dissolution {ويهلك الحرث والنسل}; (3) death {إن امرؤ هلك}. The \"هلاك عظيم\" here is the third sense compounded with the second—spiritual death through corruption of one's state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: falling into destruction (المهالك). Metaphorical: \"مفازة تهلك فيها الأرواح\" — a desert waste wherein souls perish; \"تهالك على الشيء\" — to be consumed with craving for a thing. The text's warning against \"السكون إليهم هلكة\" (settling into ease with creation is perdition) employs the metaphorical register of the soul's ruin.\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transforming a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — to turn a thing about, to divert it from one aspect to another. The heart (قلب) is so named for its constant turning and fluctuation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: turning a garment, overturning a vessel. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — a man who turns matters over and devises stratagems. In this text, the heart's turning (تقلب) between states is the very arena of the seeker's trial—whether it settles upon God or is overturned by vain imaginings.\n- Root: الي (a-l-h / ألَهَ)\n  * Kitab al-Ayn: \"المألكة: الرسالة، لأنها تؤلك في الفم\" — the message is \"chewed\" in the mouth, i.e., delivered. The root carries the sense of bearing a message between parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملائكة تقع على الواحد والجمع... الله يصطفي من الملائكة رسلا\" — angels as messengers. In this text, the preacher's self-conception as \"واسطة بين الله تعالى وبين الخلق\" (an intermediary between God and creation) is precisely the angelic office—yet the seeker is warned against mistaking satanic suggestion for genuine prophetic mission.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated conditional constructions \"فإن لم يكن... بل يرده\" and \"لا يمكن ذلك إلا بأن يخلو... ولا يخلو إلا بطول المجاهدة\" employ restrictive exception (الاستثناء) to delimit the sole means of achieving the goal, and conditional protasis-apodosis (الشروط) to map the branching perils of the path.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "18": [{"arabic": "الكتب السالفة فقال {إن هذا لفي الصحف الأولى صحف إبراهيم وموسى} فهذا منهاج رياضة المريد وتربيته في التدريج إلى لقاء الله تعالى فأما تفصيل الرياضة في كل صفة فسيأتي فإن أغلب الصفات على الإنسان بطنه وفرجه ولسانه أعني به الشهوات المتعلقة بها ثم الغضب الذي هو كالجند لحماية الشهوات ثم مهما أحب الإنسان شهوة البطن والفرج وأنس بهما أحب الدنيا ولم يتمكن منها إلا بالمال والجاه وإذا طلب المال والجاه حدث فيه الكبر والعجب والرياسة وإذا ظهر ذلك لم تسمح نفسه بترك الدنيا رأسا وتمسك من الدين بما فيه الرياسة وغلب عليه الغرور فل", "english": "Whoever devotes himself exclusively to remembrance (dhikr) and casts off the distracting attachments from his heart will not be free of such thoughts, for he has embarked upon a ship of peril. If he is saved, he becomes one of the kings of religion; if he errs, he becomes one of the perished. For this reason the Prophet (peace and blessing be upon him) said: \"Upon you is the religion of the old women\" {«عليكم بدين العجائز»}—that is, receiving the foundation of faith and the outward creed by way of blind acceptance (taqlīd), and occupying oneself with works of goodness. For the peril in deviating from that is great.", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 637", "section_title_en": "The Way of Spiritual Discipline: On the Perils of the Path and the Training of the Seeker (Section 637)", "section_index": 637, "anchors": "- Root: طرق (ṭ-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الطريق: السبيل الذي يطرق بالأرجل\" — the path beaten by feet; striking, knocking (as of a door at night).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطريق: السبيل الذي يطرق بالأرجل... وعنه استعير كل مسلك يسلكه الإنسان في فعل\" — every course a man pursues, whether praiseworthy or blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: striking iron with a hammer, knocking at a door. Metaphorical: \"طرقنا فلان طروقا... وطرقني الخيال\" — a visitor coming by night; \"أصابته طارقة من الطوارق\" — calamities that strike suddenly. Here, the \"مهالك الطريق\" are the perilous passes of the spiritual path that strike the seeker unawares.\n- Root: هلك (h-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perishing, destruction, ruin.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الهلاك على ثلاثة أوجه\" — three modes: (1) loss of a thing from one's possession while it exists elsewhere {هلك عني سلطانيه}; (2) corruption and dissolution {ويهلك الحرث والنسل}; (3) death {إن امرؤ هلك}. The \"هلاك عظيم\" here is the third sense compounded with the second—spiritual death through corruption of one's state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: falling into destruction (المهالك). Metaphorical: \"مفازة تهلك فيها الأرواح\" — a desert waste wherein souls perish; \"تهالك على الشيء\" — to be consumed with craving for a thing. The text's warning against \"السكون إليهم هلكة\" (settling into ease with creation is perdition) employs the metaphorical register of the soul's ruin.\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transforming a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — to turn a thing about, to divert it from one aspect to another. The heart (قلب) is so named for its constant turning and fluctuation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: turning a garment, overturning a vessel. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — a man who turns matters over and devises stratagems. In this text, the heart's turning (تقلب) between states is the very arena of the seeker's trial—whether it settles upon God or is overturned by vain imaginings.\n- Root: الي (a-l-h / ألَهَ)\n  * Kitab al-Ayn: \"المألكة: الرسالة، لأنها تؤلك في الفم\" — the message is \"chewed\" in the mouth, i.e., delivered. The root carries the sense of bearing a message between parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملائكة تقع على الواحد والجمع... الله يصطفي من الملائكة رسلا\" — angels as messengers. In this text, the preacher's self-conception as \"واسطة بين الله تعالى وبين الخلق\" (an intermediary between God and creation) is precisely the angelic office—yet the seeker is warned against mistaking satanic suggestion for genuine prophetic mission.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated conditional constructions \"فإن لم يكن... بل يرده\" and \"لا يمكن ذلك إلا بأن يخلو... ولا يخلو إلا بطول المجاهدة\" employ restrictive exception (الاستثناء) to delimit the sole means of achieving the goal, and conditional protasis-apodosis (الشروط) to map the branching perils of the path.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Ethics"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 250, 1272], [2, 1272, 1906], [3, 1906, 2876], [4, 2876, 4530]], "87:1": [[1, 250, 1272], [2, 1272, 1906], [3, 1906, 2876], [4, 2876, 4530]], "2": [[1, 270, 1266], [2, 1266, 1961], [3, 1961, 4130]], "87:2": [[1, 270, 1266], [2, 1266, 1961], [3, 1961, 4130]], "3": [[1, 310, 1812], [2, 1812, 2